WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.320
I think it is harder to succeed
when you're disabled.

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.600
The odds are stacked against you.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.920
It's harder to get into school,
a local school.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.160
It's harder to go to university,
it's harder to get a job.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:20.160
So really success is far further
away when you're starting out.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.000
However, when you have a disability
you learn certain things,

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.880
like resilience and communication

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.000
just because it's how you get by
in day to day life.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.640
And if you're able to harness
those in the right direction

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:35.720
and the right way

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.760
then I think that enables you
to be successful.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:55.920
My first tip is...

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.560
I can remember watching Michael Palin
on the TV and thinking, 

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.880
"Wow, I'd love to just travel
the world like him."

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.520
When I was at university,

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.960
I decided I would go for a trip
on the other side of the world.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.800
I can remember being at Sydney Bridge
and looking over the harbour 

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.200
and having goosebumps on my arms
that I was there, I'd done it.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.520
And it was such an amazing
feeling of achievement.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.080
I've not been able
to stop travelling ever since.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.720
I'm definitely
not suggesting that everyone

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.720
should do the kind of
adventures I've done

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.600
but I think it's very important
for people

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.320
to push their own comfort zone

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.640
because it just gives
you so much confidence

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.360
and a feeling that you can
achieve anything

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.440
and you can put that back
into your day to day life as well.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.400
So my next tip is...

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:43.440
...a day.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:46.480
Creativity is like a muscle
and you have to train it.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.600
Every morning I write
my 10 ideas on my phone.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.400
I write down the numbers one to 10,
I pick the topic.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.320
That could be about business

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.880
or it could be about how to improve
my health and wellbeing

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.480
or it could just be about
how to have some fun tomorrow.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.520
The point of the exercise
is just to come up with ideas

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.640
and there is no pressure
or expectation

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.320
to actually use any of them.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.200
The first five normally
come really quickly

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.720
but as you're starting to get
near the end it gets really hard

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.880
and that's a bit like
going to the gym,

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.000
you know that you're stretching
the muscle and improving it.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:24.800
...is my next tip.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.800
Good communication is key
to all relationships in life,

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.360
personal and professional.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:36.440
So with my disability sometimes
it's difficult doing handshakes

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.600
and there's been some
quite awkward moments 

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.920
where people think
I'm leaving them hanging

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.720
and it's just a bit uncomfortable.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.640
But I've learned to say,
quite directly,

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.480
please can you shake
my left hand

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:48.680
because that just enables me

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.120
not to have to take my other hand
off the joystick.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.040
So I would say overall,

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.720
you don't ever have to be rude
or nasty to people

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.920
but you should
always speak your truth.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:01.120
My final tip is to...

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.040
Having a good sense of humour
is a powerful tool

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.840
and it breaks down lots of barriers.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.120
Particularly in my life,
there are definitely times

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.040
where people see the wheelchair
before they see me

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.720
and I could get angry by that

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.320
or I could try to be
very educating and serious.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.840
So I find that humour is a good way
of putting them at ease

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:28.120
and opening up a more normal
and relaxed dialogue.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.120
When I look back on the achievements
it's quite surreal.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:33.760
It sometimes has felt,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.840
the last couple of years,
that my life's been like a movie.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.240
So I think there's a bit of...

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.840
it's still sinking in
to some degree as well.

